e in you  the more of an input-based activity the  speaking is  but it still can be quite painful  uh if you are with people and you simply  don't understand  anything i guess there are people who  believe in this  speak from day one and who practice it i  don't know how that works  uh i have been you know uh had  tutor engagements at an early stage of  learning a language and i find it  painful i can't wait till the session is  over  because i can't really understand what  they're saying and i can't find any  words  at least if i have learned the words  through my input  activities and can't remember them at  least i have a hope of eventually  remembering them but if i have no idea  what these words are and then they are  given to me in the class  there's no way i can remember them so of  course one of the things that i do  in my uh sessions with tutors is i ask  them  the tutors to produce a  text file with a list of 10 or 15 or  however many words they can  ideally they do it during the lesson so  it doesn't become an extra 10  15 minutes work for them all the words  can't be all but of the words that i  used incorrectly or couldn't remember or  wasn't able to use  or phrases if they can create a list of  these  10 15 20 and in an ideal scenario  record them then send them to me then i  import these as lessons into link and  and you know i can then review these  over and over again i can review  uh you know these reports from a lesson  two months prior and gradually  get to know these words and start to use  them  but so the talking it's important to  talk  i think it's exciting now that i've  spoken to a couple of tutors in iran  recently  one lives in espahan one lives by the  caspian sea  see there again she gave me we must have  mentioned the name of the city where she  lives  three or four times i haven't seen the  it written out yet  la john maybe i can't remember it you  know  we have to recognize that it's not just  because i'm old you can't remember stuf